{
  "dom_id": "step-q150890",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the quadratic equation ${x}^{2}+2x-15=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-15.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(-15) \\\\  & =4+60 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $64={8}^{2}$ is a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is a nonzero perfect square, the equation must have two rational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
